**** AIMEDIQ Test SRT DESC

STUDY METHOD

***** Name
Respiratory therapy based on short-term intermittent exposure to hypoxia with individual dosing of the load (Reoxy therapy)
***** Description
Reoxy therapy is a method of hypoxic conditioning based on short-term exposure to moderate hypoxia with individual dosing of the intensity of hypoxic load.
The patient is supplied through a breathing mask in an interval (intermittent) mode with a nitrogen-oxygen gas mixture with varying (from 10 vol.% to 40 vol.%) oxygen content. The supply of a hypoxic mixture (hypoxic load) alternates with the supply of an oxygenated gas mixture (reduction). Thus, one cycle of the procedure consists of “hypoxic” and oxygenated intervals, the duration of which is set individually for each patient from 1 to 6 minutes. The average number of cycles in one procedure is 5-8.
Before the first procedure, the body's ability to adapt to hypoxia will be assessed by conducting a modified hypoxic test (HT-M).
HT-M is a functional two-phase stress test based on short-term inhalation through a face mask of normobaric isocapnic nitrogen-oxygen gas mixtures with varying (from 10 vol.% to 40 vol.%) oxygen content to assess the individual response of the body to hypoxic exposure (desaturation phase) and features of recovery during the reoxygenation phase. Adapted for all categories of patients, including patients with severely reduced functionality
The purpose of testing is to calculate individual parameters of the procedure
HT-M parameters:
- O2 concentration in the hypoxic nitrogen-oxygen gas mixture – 11%
- O2 concentration in the hyperoxic nitrogen-oxygen gas mixture in the recovery phase (oxygenation) – 35-40%
- maximum duration of the hypoxic phase (time of supply of the hypoxic gas mixture) - 10 minutes
- duration of the hyperoxic phase (time of supply of the hyperoxic gas mixture) – until the initial parameters of PR and SpO2 are restored
***** Calculation of individual parameters of the IGGT procedure
Given the high variability of the response to the effects of hypoxia*, the individual target SpO2 level (SpO2 t), corresponding to the minimum SpO2 value recorded during the hypoxic test, will be used as the main parameter.

A number of authors have shown that different patients, with equal parameters of external hypoxic exposure, can experience different levels of endogenous hypoxic load, as evidenced by differences in changes in SpO2 levels. This is due to the fact that the body’s adaptive response to hypoxia is determined both by the current physiological state of the body and by individual sensitivity to oxygen deficiency, which largely depends on genotype, age, the presence of concomitant diseases, medication use and other factors. 
Therefore, the level of hypoxic load will be calculated strictly individually, based on the adaptive capabilities of each patient’s body at the time of the procedure:

- The time required for desaturation (dT) from baseline SpO2 to trough level and the time required to recover to baseline SpO2 (rT) will be used to calculate the duration of hypoxic and hyperoxic intervals in a single cycle. This study will set the threshold for dT at 10 minutes or lower to maintain a fixed degree of homogeneity among participants.
- The O2 concentration in the hypoxic gas mixture will be maintained at the level required to achieve SpO2 t and will not change throughout the entire duration of the procedure.
- The O2 concentration in the hyperoxic gas mixture will be the same for all patients and will be 35-40 vol. %
- The total duration of the procedure is 45-60 minutes, while the total cumulative duration of hypoxic exposure for 1 single procedure is 30 +/- 5 minutes.

***** Methodology
****** Hypoxic test
Before the test begins, baseline levels are measured
heart rate (min-1), SpO2 (%), SBP and DBP
The patient inhales nitrogen-oxygen gas mixtures through a tightly pressed face mask, which remains on the face throughout the test.
Registration of PE and SpO2 occurs throughout the entire test using pulse oximetry (finger sensor)
When you press the test start button, a hypoxic gas mixture is supplied to the mask.
The hypoxic phase of the test ends automatically when one of the criteria is reached:
•	increase in emergency situation by 30% or more
•	decrease in SpO2 below 83%
•	after 10 minutes (preset duration of the hypoxic phase)
•	subjective criteria: dizziness, nausea, feeling of shortness of breath, shortness of breath, pain in the heart or other subjective sensations of deterioration
Upon reaching the first three criteria, the phase change (switching to the supply of a hyperoxic gas mixture to the mask) occurs automatically. When the above subjective sensations appear, the hypoxic phase is completed manually.
After restoration of the initial SpO2 level and state of emergency, the test is considered completed. The patient removes the mask and pulse oximeter sensor.

If during the test the SpO2 level does not fall below 90%, the test results are considered not applicable for use as parameters for the subsequent procedure. The test must be repeated, reducing the O2 concentration in the inhaled gas mixture by 1%.
Before the 4th and 7th IHHT procedures, repeated testing is carried out to correct the hypoxic load parameters
****** Reoxy therapy procedure
Before starting the procedure, the baseline levels of heart rate (min-1), SpO2 (%), SBP and DBP are measured
The patient inhales nitrogen-oxygen gas mixtures through a tightly pressed face mask, which remains on the face throughout the procedure.
Registration of emergency and SpO2 occurs throughout the entire procedure using pulse oximetry (finger sensor)
When you press the button to start the procedure, a hypoxic gas mixture is supplied to the mask.
Switching of gas flows will occur automatically according to the specified parameters.
The procedure ends automatically after a preset time has elapsed. The patient removes the mask and pulse oximeter sensor.
After the procedure is completed, the levels of SBP and DBP are measured again
***** Mode
The total duration of the procedure is 45-60 minutes
5 times per week
2 hours before PT
***** Providing a method
To carry out HT-M and the reoxy therapy procedure, a Reoxy respiratory therapy unit with a built-in “hypoxic test” program will be used.
During an IHHT session, the software automatically adjusts the switching of the gas mixture depending on changes in physiological parameters (blood oxygen saturation and heart rate) in response to hypoxic and hyperoxic exposure (biofeedback control).
***** Epidemiological safety
To ensure sanitary and hygienic requirements, the ReOxy respiratory therapy unit used in the study has a multi-stage filtration and purification system for the supplied gas mixture, as well as an external port (connector) for connecting an individual breathing circuit through an antibacterial / antiviral filter, providing protection against viral and bacterial contamination both the patient and the device. The use of a two-port mask as part of an individual breathing circuit ensures a unidirectional flow of the gas mixture from the device to the patient, exhalation is carried out into the environment.





